    No such obligation attaches to libm0core itself: m0-core has no
    dependencies and none of this code is in it.

    Forked from upstream release v26.1.2 (commit 3aae65f46a72, tagged
    2026-02-22). Upstream was archived on 2026-05-12 and is no longer
    maintained; the copy in this repository is a permanent hard fork, not a
    vendored snapshot tracking an upstream branch.

    MODIFIED. This copy diverges substantially from upstream v26.1.2 and must
    not be assumed interchangeable with it. Changes were made first in the
    private mojo-siren-grail repository (28 commits, from 2026-02-26) and
    continued in the private mojo-framework monorepo (15 commits, from
    2026-03-18), from which this repository was extracted. The modifications
    include:

      - Security hardening against request smuggling, slowloris, and integer
        overflow in request parsing
      - Production hardening: connection timeouts, observability hooks,
        response caching, concurrency limits
      - epoll_event struct layout corrected on non-x86_64 targets; accept
        queue drain fix
      - Nullable epoll/timerfd FFI parameters modeled as Optional
      - Null UnsafePointer construction via unsafe_from_address=0
      - Forward compatibility with Mojo 1.1; migration off deprecated
        memory/origin APIs
      - uri.mojo: implicit `hashlib` stdlib import corrected to `std.hashlib`,
        which any consumer importing the compiled package needed
      - uri.mojo: `URI.parse` decided a target was an absolute URI by
        searching the WHOLE string for `://`, so a request target carrying an
        unencoded URL in a query parameter (`/go?url=http://x`) was parsed as
        a URI whose scheme was `/go?url=http` and answered 400 before
        reaching the application. `scheme_separator` now accepts the `://`
        only when everything before it is a scheme as RFC 3986 §3.1 defines
        one (ALPHA, then ALPHA/DIGIT/`+`/`-`/`.`), which by construction
        excludes `/`, `?` and `#`. Tested in test_uri_scheme.mojo
      - uri.mojo: `URI.parse` skipped a userinfo at the first `@` anywhere
        after the scheme, so an unencoded `@` in a request's path or query
        (`/echo?x=a@b`) discarded the path and query and the request
        reached `/`, and a client URL was dialled at whatever followed an
        `@` in its query. `userinfo_separator` accepts the `@` only inside
        the authority, which RFC 3986 §3.2 ends at the first `/`, `?` or
        `#`. Tested in test_uri_userinfo.mojo
      - uri.mojo: `unquote` decoded by slicing its input as a String
        (`[byte=a:b]`), which asserts a codepoint boundary, so a request
        target holding a byte that is not UTF-8 beside a percent-escape
        trapped the loop thread: one `GET /?x=<0x80>%41` killed the
        process, every app alike. It now walks the input as bytes into one
        output buffer -- the decoder is a byte operation -- and requires
        exactly two hex digits for an escape where `atol` accepted a sign.
        Tested in test_unquote.mojo
      - cookie/request_cookie_jar.mojo: `add_pairs` sliced each pair as a
        String around its `=`, the same codepoint-boundary assert, and the
        jar is built for every request -- so `Cookie: a=<0x80>` killed the
        process on the loop thread for every application. Byte-span slices
        now. Tested in test_request_cookies.mojo
      - header.mojo: `Headers.keys()` added, so headers can be enumerated
        without reaching into the private `_inner` dict from another package
        (needed to project them into a WSGI environ)
      - header.mojo: `name_span`/`value_span` made public (they were
        `_name_span`/`_value_span`). `keys()` + `get()` is the allocating
        way to enumerate headers — a String per name, another per value, and
        a linear scan per lookup — which measured 48us per request
        projecting twelve headers into the WSGI blob, 77% of that bridge's
        whole per-request cost. Walking `count()` with these two spans
        allocates nothing; the projection now runs in 0.44us
      - Hot-path allocation pass, profile-ranked (a flat sample of the
        hello row put allocator traffic at 29% of busy time): Headers'
        four parallel Int32 index arrays became one packed stride-4 array
        (a Headers is built fresh for every request AND response, so this
        removed six allocations per request); HTTPResponse's three
        constructors take headers/cookies by `var` and move instead of
        `.copy()` (the arguments are almost always inline temporaries —
        source-compatible, a caller that reuses a named value gets an
        implicit copy); Headers.set_int added and both set_content_length
        implementations plus the status-code write in encode use integer
        formatting into the writer rather than a per-call String. Together
        +25% on the hello row (92.4k -> 115.2k rps) and +20% through the
        WSGI bridge, byte-identical responses verified
      - http/parsing.mojo: the SIMD scanners rewritten around one idiom. The
        originals learned with a `reduce_min` that a 64-byte chunk held a
        match and then walked its lanes with a scalar loop to find which
        (9.4 ns per chunk); a `select` of `iota` and a second `reduce_min`
        names the lane in 0.8 ns. Each scanner now searches its whole
        remaining input — 64 lanes wide, then 16, then scalar — instead of
        complete 64-byte chunks with a `try_peek`-per-byte tail. One answer
        corrected on the way: the value scanner's CR-first three-stage
        check reported the first CR even when an LF or NUL preceded it in
        the chunk, so a bare-LF line ending followed by a CR within 64
        bytes swallowed the next header line; the mask is now the scalar
        tail's own predicate. Tested by length sweeps and a bare-LF case
        in test_parsing.mojo
      - header.mojo: `Headers.reserve` added and `set_bytes` copies a value
        with one `extend`; the parse wrappers size the blob from the bytes
        consumed and leave the 100-entry offsets array uninitialized (every
        entry is fully written before it is counted). With the scanners
        above, `parse_request_headers` 1.96 -> 0.86 us on the twelve-header
        browser GET and the whole user-space request 3.33 -> 1.97 us
      - event_loop.mojo: the graceful drain's between-requests sweep runs
        after every completion pass, not once before the drain clock
        starts. A response completing during the drain went out in one
        send, registered no write interest, and re-armed its keep-alive
        slot for a request the drain never reads; the process then waited
        the whole 5 s budget for an answered connection (5.35 s against
        1.55 s with Connection: close, measured). Pinned by
        scripts/drain_inflight_probe.py in smoke-shutdown
      - event_loop.mojo / offload.mojo: the per-pass outbox sweep over all
        slots runs only while a slot streams (`OffloadLoopState.
        streaming_hint`, an upper bound raised where a stream flag is set
        and recounted by the sweep), except under the pump executor, whose
        loop keeps sweeping every pass (`OffloadPool.sweep_every_pass`)
        because the 1.2 us the sweep costs there measured as load-bearing
        pacing for its submit batching (-3% rps without it at c16). hello
        +3.3% at c16, the inverted executor +4.6%, the pump at parity
      - fcntl F_SETFL fixed on ARM64 macOS: fcntl is variadic, Darwin
        passes variadic arguments on the stack while external_call used
        registers, so set_nonblocking silently did nothing there for the
        fork's whole life. The call now pads to nine fixed arguments on
        Darwin, forcing the flag argument onto the stack where the callee's
        va_list reads it; a regression test asserts F_GETFL reflects
        O_NONBLOCK. The visible symptom was a multi-worker accept race:
        the losing worker blocked inside accept() on the still-blocking
        shared listener, wedging its event loop
      - websocket.mojo added (no upstream counterpart): RFC 6455 server
        side — opening handshake (self-contained SHA-1 and base64),
        frame encode/decode with client-masking enforcement, fragmented
        message assembly, ping/pong and close handshake; the event loop
        gained a STREAMING_WS slot state, frame-mode reads after a 101
        upgrade response, WebSocket heartbeat pings on the SSE heartbeat
        cadence, and a ws_message hook on HTTPService
      - websocket.mojo: client-frame unmasking and UTF-8 validation
        vectorized. Unmasking splatted the frame's 4-byte mask across 64
        lanes instead of applying it a byte at a time with an index-modulo
        (measured 3.0x at a 64-byte payload, 56.9x at 8 KB, 77.9x at
        64 KB); text validation skips pure-ASCII runs 64 bytes at a time,
        leaving every non-ASCII byte to the same strict scalar decoder
        (16x at 64 bytes, 56x at 8 KB, and neutral on mostly-multi-byte
        text). Several byte-at-a-time buffer copies in the frame parser
        became bulk extends
      - Migration off the memory/origin APIs Mojo 1.0 deprecates, across the
        fork: UnsafePointer -> Pointer, ImmutOrigin -> ImmOrigin,
        Mut/ImmutExternalOrigin -> *UntrackedOrigin, pointer arithmetic
        `(p + i).m()` -> `p.unsafe_offset(i).m()`, `.load`/`.bitcast`/`.free`
        and the init_pointee_*/take_pointee/destroy_pointee family to their
        `unsafe_` spellings, `__del__` -> `__deinit__`,
        ImplicitlyDeletable/Destructible -> Deinitable. Behaviour-preserving
        renames; the exception is `alloc` without a Layout, which is
        deprecated in favour of an `unsafe_alloc` the pinned toolchain does
        not actually export (12 call sites left as they are)
      - Header read timeout corrected and moved off timerfds. The deadline
        was re-stamped after every response, so any keep-alive connection
        idle longer than header_read_timeout answered 408 to the next
        request the client sent — promptly and correctly — even though
        idle_timeout (60s, vs 10s) was what governed the gap. It is now
        stamped when a request's bytes actually start arriving, and cleared
        between requests. The per-connection timerfd it used is gone: the
        once-a-second sweep that already ran for idle timeouts owns the
        deadline, which also covers a client stalling midway through headers
        on a reused connection (the timerfd was armed on accept and retired
        at the first complete header parse, so it never did)
      - Connection provisions size their buffers on first use instead of at
        pool construction. The pool builds every provision up front
        (max_connections, 1024 by default) and each held three buffers at
        socket_buffer_size, so a server allocated ~12 MB of resident memory
        for its worst case before accepting anything, multiplied per
        prefork worker. Provisions are never reconstructed, so a slot keeps
        its buffers once used and the peak now tracks the high-water mark of
        concurrent connections: 26.4 -> 14.0 MB idle, and 77.7 -> 28.0 MB at
        four workers
      - Client-side connect path (create_connection, getaddrinfo bindings)
        revived after rotting as dead code: addrinfo pointer fields modeled
        as Optional[Pointer] (Mojo 1.0 pointers are non-nullable), connect()
        fd type corrected, create_connection propagates the original error
        (its typed re-wrap never compiled), getaddrinfo called with a valid
        numeric service instead of a non-NULL empty string glibc rejects,
        and ai_protocol no longer passed an address family
      - The blocking accept loop refuses streaming responses with 409
        (gate_streaming_response): an sse_streaming response or a 101
        upgrade needs event-loop machinery the blocking path does not have,
        and used to be written as a silent one-shot body instead
      - http/common_response.mojo imports the names it uses (HTTPResponse,
        Headers, Header, HeaderKey, Cookie, ResponseCookieJar) from their
        defining modules instead of resolving them through the enclosing
        http/__init__ star-import. That implicit-reference pattern is
        deprecated in Mojo 1.0 and accounted for 57 of the repository's
        143 unique compiler warnings. Nothing imports the enclosing http
        package itself, so the star-import that pulls common_response in
        is not re-entered and no cycle is introduced
      - c/process.mojo gained signal(2) as install_signal_handler, next to
        the kill/SIGTERM helpers already there. sigaction(2) was avoided
        deliberately: struct sigaction has no portable layout (16 bytes on
        macOS, 152 on glibc) and nothing here needs a flag signal(2) cannot
        reach. waitpid_blocking now retries on EINTR rather than raising —
        reachable for the first time now that the supervisor catches SIGTERM
      - offload.mojo (new) and the event-loop changes behind it: the loop
        can hand a parsed request to a pool of handler threads instead of
        calling HTTPService.func itself. Two SOCK_DGRAM socketpairs — a
        submit channel N threads receive on, and a completion channel the
        loop registers with its backend exactly as it registers a broadcast
        bus channel — plus per-slot request/response storage the two sides
        hand between them, each handoff published by the socketpair syscall
        that names it. run_event_loop gained an offload_addr argument
        (0 = the behaviour it always had), and _process_request was split so
        the synchronous path and the completion path converge on ONE
        implementation of the wire rules rather than growing a second copy.
        A slot with a job in flight is not touched by the loop and is not
        recycled by it: a client that vanishes meanwhile detaches the fd but
        leaves the provision borrowed, which is what _close_slot's new
        release_provision=False is for. The HTTPService trait is unchanged,
        which was a constraint rather than an accident
      - http/chunked.mojo gained an ENCODER beside its decoder
        (append_chunk_size, encode_chunk, chunked_terminator), and the
        event loop uses it to frame streaming responses on HTTP/1.1:
        Transfer-Encoding: chunked on the head, size CRLF payload CRLF per
        drain, and 0 CRLF CRLF at end-of-stream, after which the connection
        returns to keep-alive instead of closing. Before this, every
        streamed body was close-delimited and a stream therefore ended its
        connection. Framing is refused where it would corrupt rather than
        differ — HTTP/1.0, HEAD, a 101 upgrade — and is limited to the
        executor's ASGI streams. Two accounting rules came with it: the
        outbox drain reads sse_is_streaming once per pass (the drain is
        what flips it), and stream credit acks count payload rather than
        wire bytes, which is why OffloadLoopState carries ack_payload
        alongside the new http11 and chunked slot arrays
      - c/sendfile.mojo (new) and fd-backed response bodies: HTTPResponse
        gained body_fd/body_fd_offset/body_fd_len as an alternative to
        body_raw, and both write paths learned to transfer it with
        sendfile(2) — the event loop across readiness events, the blocking
        loop in a plain loop. The file's bytes never enter the process.
        Darwin and Linux disagree about the argument order and about how a
        short write is reported (Darwin returns -1/EAGAIN WITH a positive
        count), which is what SendFileResult normalises. The descriptor is
        owned by the ConnectionProvision once the head is encoded, and
        released by close_body_fd on every path — completion, client
        disconnect, and HEAD stripping — so a client that vanishes
        mid-transfer cannot leak it
      - c/socketpair.mojo (new) holds the socketpair(2) binding that
        broadcast.mojo had privately. Both callers want an AF_UNIX
        SOCK_DGRAM pair for the same reason (message boundaries, so
        concurrent writers cannot interleave and concurrent readers each
        dequeue one whole message) and differ only in what they do with the
        ends afterwards, so the helper returns raw descriptors and sets no
        options. Behaviour-preserving; it also keeps the deprecated-`alloc`
        warning count at one site instead of two
      - c/pipe.mojo gained ShutdownHandle.notify(), a one-byte write(2) for
        signal handlers to call, and close_fd() for unwinding a half-built
        pipe. notify() exists because signal() closes the write end, and a
        close is not safe to repeat: a second signal would close an fd the
        kernel may have already recycled onto a live connection. The byte it
        writes is sourced from .rodata, not a local — the buffer address
        reaches external_call as an untracked pointer, which severs the alias
        information and lets the store to a local be dead-coded (verified: it
        wrote an uninitialised 0x00)
      - Remaining Mojo 1.0 deprecations cleared across the fork: positional
        pointer indexing `p[i]` -> `p[unsafe_offset=i]` (18 sites in
        c/epoll.mojo, c/epoll_backend.mojo and c/kqueue_backend.mojo),
        memcpy -> unsafe_memcpy in event_loop.mojo, and server_config.mojo's
        `__init__(*, deinit take:)` -> `__init__(*, deinit move:)`, which is
        the spelling the compiler now requires for a move constructor. Also
        drops a dead `var slot = UNUSED` initialiser in event_loop.mojo's
        accept path: every reader is preceded by the borrow that assigns it,
        and the failure branch closes the fd and continues. Behaviour-
        preserving throughout. Two deprecations remain unfixable rather
        than unfixed, both warning about APIs Mojo 1.0.0 does not ship:
        `alloc` without a Layout (12 sites here) and `ABI="C"` on
        @export (in m0-core, outside the fork)
      - Three latent name/deprecation faults fixed, each only reachable when
        the import graph is entered from a particular direction and so
        invisible to every build until one was: cookie/request_cookie_jar.mojo
        used `Headers` in parse_cookies without importing it (it happened to
        resolve through the header <-> http.parsing cycle on the usual entry
        path), and uri.mojo's __str__ and is_http still called
        `len(String)`, which Mojo 1.0 rejects outright. Bodies elaborate on
        demand, so all three compiled cleanly until a consumer reached them.
        Behaviour-preserving; byte_length() is what len() meant

      - accept() passed a 4-byte addrlen (sizeof(socklen_t) -- the TODO in
        the code flagged it), so the kernel truncated the peer sockaddr
        before the IP bytes; the address was unreadable even in principle.
        accept_with_peer keeps the full sockaddr and parses the IPv4 peer
        (the u16 sa_family holds macOS's [len][family] byte pair, the same
        single-definition ABI hazard the fcntl fix documents); the event
        loop stamps it onto each request (HTTPRequest.remote_addr/
        remote_port, ConnectionProvision.peer_host/peer_port) so WSGI's
        REMOTE_ADDR and ASGI's scope["client"] carry the real peer.

      - offload.mojo grew a second producer on the chunk channel (a
        --blocking-threads pool thread streaming a WSGI iterable): per-slot
        ack fds (`slot_ack_fd`, `slot_channel_stream`, `make_stream_ack_pair`,
        `drain_ack_fd`), the chunk pair split from the executor's ack pair
        (`enable_base_stream_ack`, `chunk_active`), per-producer stream
        generations (`stream_gen_seed`), and a `TAG_STREAM_ABORT` datagram
        on the completion channel (`abort_stream`, `take_aborts`).
        event_loop.mojo asks the per-slot `slot_channel_stream` at its four
        stream decisions, clears the stream state at accept and at every
        stream end, drains the chunk channel inline before finishing a
        channel-stream head, handles aborts after their batch's completions
        (flushing the produced bytes, then closing without a terminator),
        and repeats the farewell pass after the shutdown drain.
        http/response.mojo gained `stream_gen`. An abort also applies to a
        WebSocket slot (`slot_sse or slot_ws`), and a 101 records its
        generation in `OffloadLoopState.stream_gen` after the non-stream
        clear, so an outbound frame the chunk channel will not take closes
        the socket rather than leaving it open with a hole in its message
        stream.
      - The pump between the event loop and the asyncio executor is
        batched in both directions (offload.mojo, event_loop.mojo): the
        loop buffers a pass's submits to an executor lane and sends them
        as one `TAG_JOB_BATCH` datagram at the bottom of the pass
        (`OffloadLoopState.queue_submit`/`flush_submits`, `submit_batch`,
        `lane_is_executor`, `stamp_lane`), running inline what a batch
        cannot carry; and `drain_completions` decodes `k` concatenated
        completions from one datagram (`complete_many`) into a buffer
        sized for a full batch.
      - ring.mojo (new), and the pool handoff in offload.mojo and
        event_loop.mojo rides it: a bounded MPMC queue (Vyukov's) per pool
        lane for jobs and one for completions, in malloc'd memory kept for
        the process's life. The socketpairs carry only wakes (`_POKE`, sent
        when the receiving side has counted itself parked), pills, inbound
        WebSocket messages, stream aborts and the executor's batches.
        `next_job` spins before it parks and polls its socket non-blocking
        once per `POOL_DGRAM_POLL_NS`; `drain_completions(read_fd=False)`
        is the syscall-free drain the loop runs at the top and bottom of
        every pass; `_wait_for_events` is the loop's announce-then-recheck
        around `backend.wait`; a thread that takes a job and leaves work
        on the ring pokes a parked sibling (`_chain_wake`), since its own
        socket poll may have consumed that sibling's wake; `_complete_one`
        drains both bus channels before finishing a streaming head or
        101, since a ring completion is not an event and its frame's
        readiness may not be in the pass. `M0_POOL_RING=0` restores the
        datagram handoff.

      - event_loop.mojo / websocket.mojo: a WebSocket the SERVER closes
        now lingers for the peer's Close reply rather than closing at
        once. RFC 6455 5.5.1 has the endpoint that sends Close first wait
        to receive one; closing immediately meant the peer's reply reached
        a socket that was gone, and the RST answering it discarded our FIN
        and, for a client far enough behind, the Close frame itself.
        `WSState.closing` marks the wait, the three stream-ended close
        sites and `_after_send`'s should_close branch set a
        `WS_CLOSE_LINGER_NS` deadline in `slot_idle_deadline` (a
        WebSocket's is otherwise 0, so a non-zero one IS the linger) and
        leave the read armed, and the read path drops the parser's close
        echo because this side already sent one.

      - connection.mojo's ListenConfig gained `reuse_port` (default False:
        SO_REUSEPORT had been set unconditionally, so a second server on a
        busy port bound beside the first) and `quiet` (suppresses the
        listening banner and the "Ready" line for a caller whose own
        startup line is the ready signal); the first bind failure names
        the address.
      - The twelve `alloc`-without-a-`Layout` sites moved to `unsafe_alloc`
        (`std.memory.alloc`), the non-Layout allocator the compiler's own
        fix-it names. Two earlier entries here record that symbol as absent
        from Mojo 1.0.0; it is importable, and with the doc-string and
        `@export` fixes outside the fork the ratchet's baseline went from 68
        to 0. Behaviour-preserving: same pointer type, same frees.
      - utils/owning_list.mojo removed (2026-09-05). It was a ~400-line copy
        of the stdlib `List` from when `List[T]` required a `Copyable`
        element, kept for the per-slot tables whose elements own buffers
        or descriptors. On Mojo 1.0 `List` and `Optional` take a
        Movable-only element, so its seven users — the loop's per-slot
        response buffers and WebSocket parsers, `ProvisionPool`, the
        offload pool's parked requests and responses, and three outside
        the fork — are plain `List` now, and the file's two `unsafe_alloc`
        sites and ASan container annotations went with it. Measured before
        and after on the hello row: 171.6k/172.7k/174.8k req/s at
