        is 48 bytes, not 56, and `msg_flags` is the high half of the sixth
        word, so the seventh, which the check read, was never written. The
        bit is now per platform and the flags are read where each kernel
        writes them. A refused message closes every descriptor that reached
        its control buffer, since the kernel installs them on receipt. A
        payload cut short keeps its descriptor.

      - server.mojo, http/common_response.mojo (2026-09-28): the blocking
        accept loop is retired. `Server.listen_and_serve` and
        `Server.serve` delegate to `listen_and_serve_nonblocking` and
        `serve_nonblocking`, so every entry point runs the event loop and
        honours the server's `shutdown_read_fd`. Removed with the loop:
        `handle_connection`, `_send_error_response`,
        `gate_streaming_response` and the 409 `StreamingUnsupported` it
        answered SSE and WebSocket responses with, and
        `ConnectionProvision`'s `request` and `response` fields, which
        nothing else used. It served one connection at a time, so one idle
        keep-alive client held every other, and it had missed fixes the
        event loop carries, the lingering close after an early 413 among
        them; nothing in the tree called it.

      - http/date.mojo (2026-09-28): `http_date_from_unix` fills a
        `struct tm` it allocates, through `gmtime_r`, where it read the one
        `gmtime` returns. On glibc that is one static buffer for the whole
        process, and every event loop formats its Date header here, so
        with loops on threads (`M0_THREADS`, `--threads`) one loop could
        read the fields another had just written.

      - c/socket_error.mojo, c/socket.mojo, socket.mojo, connection.mojo,
        server.mojo, event_loop.mojo, offload.mojo, http/response.mojo
        (2026-09-28): one error, `SysError` -- the call's name and its
        errno, with `would_block`, `interrupted`, `connection_aborted` and
        `address_in_use` -- replaces c/socket_error.mojo's 109 typed errors
        and the 14 variants over them, and every wrapper in c/socket.mojo
        raises it whenever its call fails. The wrappers raised a type for
        each errno their man page listed and returned normally for any
        other: `socket()` gave back -1 as a descriptor (EPROTOTYPE on
        macOS), `listen()` on a connected socket reported success (EINVAL),
        and `accept_with_peer` handed the event loop -1 to admit (review
        record B9). The loop and the handler pool read the predicates where
        they matched leaf types, and the listener's bind retry reads
        `address_in_use` (review record R9): no errno is handled differently.
        The listener raises the failed call's own error where it raised
        `SocketCreationError` or `ListenFailedError`, which dropped the
        errno. `TCPConnection.write` raises untyped, since the client mixes
        it with errors of its own in one `try`, which `SendError` absorbed.
        Removed with the typed errors, each without a raiser or a caller:
        `Socket.accept`, `NoTLSListener.accept` and the `accept` wrapper;
        `SocketAcceptError`, `SocketConnectError`, `SocketRecvfromError`,
        `FatalCloseError` and `InvalidCloseErrorConversionError`;
        `SocketGetsocknameError`, now `SocketNameError`, which
        `get_peer_name` raises too; `CreateConnectionError` and
        `BindFailedError`; `ServerError`'s accept, receive and close arms;
        and `ChunkedEncodingError`. About 2,960 lines.

      - accept_share.mojo, broadcast.mojo, server_config.mojo (2026-09-28):
        the hand-written copy and move constructors of `AcceptShare`,
        `BroadcastBus` and `ServerConfig` are deleted. Each copied or moved
        every field and did nothing else, which is what the compiler
        derives. Behaviour-preserving.

      - event_loop.mojo (2026-09-28): every function of the loop takes its
        state whole, as `(handler, backend, st, slot, fd)` less what it
        does not use, where it took the fields of `LoopState` one by one:
        thirteen functions took 20 to 28 parameters, and each of
        `_close_slot`'s 46 call sites spelled twelve of them (review record
        C3). `_run_pass` is the pass's outline over named blocks
        (`_on_timer`, `_heartbeat`, `_on_read`, `_read_websocket`,
        `_read_body`, `_on_write`, `_admit_batches`, `_retry_owed_acks`,
        `_drain_outboxes`, `_sweep_deadlines`, `_linger_handler_closes`,
        `_resume_suspended_reads`), 176 lines where it was 1,343, and
        `LoopState` builds its own slot tables, so `prepare_loop` only
        registers. Behaviour-preserving: the blocks moved verbatim and run
        in the same order. The file went from 4,576 lines to 3,686.

      - event_loop.mojo, loop/ (2026-09-28): the event loop is split into
        modules, the package `lightbug_http.loop` (review record C3):
        `state` (`LoopState`, the constants the modules share, and the
        per-slot transitions, `_close_slot` among them), `timers`,
        `accept`, `request`, `response`, `streams`, `offload` (the loop's
        side of the handler pool and the ASGI executor) and `shutdown`.
        `event_loop.mojo` keeps `prepare_loop`, `run_event_loop`,
        `run_pass_once` and the pass's outline, `_run_pass`, and imports
        what the ASGI executor takes from it, so no caller changed. A move:
        every function and comment went verbatim, by script, save two
        comments that pointed across the new boundaries. The fork's one
        import of `m0_http` (`m0_http.log`) now sits in `loop/state.mojo`,
        with the access log's `_record_response`, and DECISIONS D33 covers
        every module of `loop/`. The 3,686-line file is a 467-line door and
        eight modules of 139 to 946 lines.

      - offload.mojo (2026-09-28): the hand-written move constructors of
        `OffloadPool` and `OffloadLoopState` are deleted. Each moved every
        field and did nothing else, which is what the compiler derives.
        Behaviour-preserving.

      - offload.mojo, hold.mojo (2026-09-28): `OffloadPool.send_ws_message`
        is the one encoder of a pool lane's inbound WebSocket message; the
        m0-wsgi loop handler, which carried a copy of it and of its
        65546-byte bound, calls it, and `wake_for_datagram`, which existed
        for that copy, is removed. The bound is `WS_DATAGRAM_MAX` alone, and
        `ws_message_room` is the arithmetic both the encoder and the
        handler's 1009 check refuse by. `send_bounded` is the one bounded
        send, where these two files and the handler had 16 copies of the
        retry loop, and `append_i64_le` and `read_i64_le` the one i64
        codec, where they had 15 spellings. Behaviour-preserving: the same
        bytes on every channel, and the same number of tries.

      - loop/request.mojo (2026-09-28): `_read_body` re-registers read
        interest only after a read that filled the staging buffer, or on
        the peer's EOF, and otherwise keeps a registration that stands, as
        `_handle_read_headers` does: it re-registered after every read of a
        body, R2's `epoll_ctl` pair on each read of an upload still
        arriving (review record R2).

      - loop/accept.mojo (2026-09-28): the accept drain goes on past the
        network errors Linux's `accept` returns for a connection it has
        already taken off the queue (ENETDOWN, EPROTO, ENOPROTOOPT,
        EHOSTDOWN, ENONET, EHOSTUNREACH, EOPNOTSUPP, ENETUNREACH), which
        accept(2) says to retry like EAGAIN, as it goes on past
        ECONNABORTED and EINTR (`_accept_retries`); read as any other error,
        they stopped the pass.

      - c/fdpass.mojo, accept_share.mojo, loop/accept.mojo (2026-09-28):
        `recv_fd` tells a datagram that brought no descriptor to hand over
        (`RECV_FD_REFUSED`) from an empty channel (`RECV_FD_EMPTY`); both
        were -1, and the accept-share drain stopped at either, which a
        receiver at its open-file limit gets from a sender that did nothing
        wrong. The drain skips a refusal, `AcceptShare.receive` retires it
        from `pending`, and a zero-length datagram's descriptor is handed
        over rather than left open.

      - loop/state.mojo (2026-09-28): `MAX_EVENTS`, which nothing read
        (each backend sizes its own event buffer), is removed.

      - event_loop.mojo (2026-09-29): the pass that reads the shutdown pipe
        dispatches the rest of its batch, where it stopped at the pipe's
        event; it still admits no new connection. Each event behind the
        pipe is reported once -- epoll's reads, channels and writes are
        edge triggered, kqueue's writes and timers one-shots -- so on Linux
        a completion there left its request unanswered and the drain
        waiting out its 5 s (review record B22).

      - loop/shutdown.mojo (2026-09-29): the drain deregisters the listener
        and forgets its number before it closes it. On epoll a closed
        descriptor stays in the interest list while another process holds
        its open file description, which under `--workers N` the
        supervisor and every sibling do: the draining worker kept waking
        for connections waiting for its siblings, and a connection handed
        over during the drain that took the listener's freed number had
        its reads taken for the listener's, its request never read
        (review record B24).

      - event_loop.mojo, server.mojo, socket.mojo, connection.mojo
        (2026-09-29): `run_event_loop` owns the listener it is given and
        closes it once -- at the drain, or on the way out of an error
        before it -- and `Server.serve` and `serve_nonblocking` take the
        listener rather than borrowing it, handing it over with
        `NoTLSListener.into_fd` (`Socket.into_fd`), which gives up the
        descriptor without closing it. The borrowed listener's destructor
        closed the number again once the loop returned, when the drain had
        freed it and something else in the process had usually been given
        it (review record B26).

      - accept_share.mojo, loop/accept.mojo, loop/shutdown.mojo
        (2026-09-29): no accept-sharing hand-off is lost to a worker that
        is leaving. The sender reads the target's `state` again after
        raising its `pending` and keeps a connection whose target has left
        (`send_with`); the leaver's drain runs on while `pending`, less
        what it has received, is above 0 (`awaiting_handoffs`); a worker
        that has left passes what its channel delivers on to a sibling
        that has not (`forward`, `pick_for_leaver`), admitting it only when
        none is left; and the drain's tail retires what the shutdown took
        off the channel without a pass. A hand-off sent after an idle
        leaver's drain had ended was never read, and one admitted by a
        drain before its first byte was closed as idle (review record
        B25). A fourth word on each worker's line, `taken`, publishes what
        the worker has received and not yet retired, and the worker that
        takes the index of one that died subtracts it from `pending`
        (`start`): those connections died with it, and a count left
        behind would hold every later drain at the index to its budget.

      - accept_share.mojo, c/fdpass.mojo (2026-09-29): on macOS each
        accept-share channel's read end is kept in flight, in a socket pair
        of its own, for the life of the process (`_anchor_channels`). XNU's
        collector of descriptors in flight (`unp_gc`) follows only what is
        in flight, so it never followed a channel that was merely open,
        and it flushed every hand-off queued in one whenever any AF_UNIX
        socket on the machine was closed: the request discarded, the
        connection closed unanswered at its first read. And `recv_fd`
        receives there without MSG_DONTWAIT, on a non-blocking channel, so
        a receive waits out the collector's scan of the channel, which
        holds the buffer's lock, instead of failing EAGAIN with a datagram
        queued (review record B25).

      - c/network.mojo (2026-09-29): `inet_pton` counts its address buffer
        in bytes. It was `stack_allocation[4, c_void]` (16 for AF_INET6),
        and `c_void` is `NoneType`, whose size is zero, so every listen
        wrote the address past a buffer of no bytes onto the caller's stack
        (review record B30).

      - socket.mojo (2026-09-29): `Socket.close()` marks the socket
        unconnected, and does nothing on a socket already closed. A socket
        made from a descriptor (`Socket(fd=...)`) is born connected, so
        destroyed after `close()` it shut down the number it had closed,
        and a second `close()` closed it again -- by then usually another
        descriptor's, a new one taking the lowest free number (review
        record B27).

      - hold.mojo (2026-09-29): `take_hold` strips `M0-Channel` on every
        response it runs on, an orphan one included -- it returned before
        the pop when `M0-Hold` was absent, so a response carrying only
        `M0-Channel` (a leftover, or an `M0-Hold` the bridge dropped for a
        control byte) leaked an internal instruction header to the client;
        and it refuses a channel in the reserved `\x01` namespace as it
        refuses a missing one, the namespace `channel_is_reserved` guards at
        every other publish boundary, since `%01` in a form field decodes to
        a real 0x01 the bridge does not drop and such a name addresses a
        connection slot on the loop (review record Hold).

      - socket.mojo (2026-09-29): `Socket.close()` leaves the socket
        holding -1 in place of the number it closed, so every later call
        -- `send`, `receive`, `bind`, `listen`, `connect`, the socket
        options -- fails with EBADF, `shutdown` does nothing and `into_fd`
        returns -1, where each acted on whatever descriptor had taken the
        number since (review record B27b).

      - offload.mojo (2026-09-29): `OffloadPool.stop` waits for room to
        pill a thread that parks on its lane's socket -- an executor, a
        test's thread, every pool thread under `M0_POOL_ELASTIC=0` or
        without rings -- rather than offering the pill once and ignoring a
        refusal (`_offer_until`, `PILL_WAIT_NS`). Inbound WebSocket
        messages fill that socket while every thread of the lane is inside
        a view; the thread came back, took them, and parked on an empty
        socket for good, which a bounded join abandoned and an unbounded
        one never returned from. The wait shares its caller's join
        deadline (`stop_deadline`, `ns_left`), so the two are one bound
        (review record C9b-c).

      - address.mojo, socket.mojo, c/socket.mojo, connection.mojo
        (2026-09-29): the rest of the upstream surface no caller used,
        removed as the 2026-09-28 removal was: `NetworkType.udp4` and
        `udp6`, the free functions `is_ip_protocol`, `is_ipv4` and
        `is_ipv6` (the `NetworkType` methods of those names stay), the
        `TCP4Socket` and `TCP6Socket` aliases, `O_ACCMODE`, and the
